{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The identity probe executed: receipt bd165f9c43d94a7c9b57791a1979c00b (exit 0, its single test passed) parsed event 1 `overwritten_index_resolution` {a_decided_idx:1, a_entry_at_index_1_is_own:false, b_decided_idx:1, b_idx:1, resolution:\\\"Ok(1)\\\"} — node 1's append_notify for its own entry resolved Ok(1) while index 1 on node 1 holds node 2's entry, which node 2's own call was assigned. Together with the sources (index-based arm matched before the ballot-based Superseded arm; Superseded's own doc admitting the entry can be lost; the Ok contract of 'await its decided log index') that is the third finding: the completion endpoint can report success for an entry that was never decided at the reported index, shadowing the heuristic written for exactly that leadership change. The account is recorded as explained with defect_claim asserted and explicitly notes the remaining premise supplied by the construction (node 1 really held its own entry and node 2's log was empty) plus the limits (constructed interleaving, contract reading of Ok, no frequency claim). Formal support is withheld because that receipt records parsing_error_count 1 — the companion `after_b_takeover` line rendered Rust's `{:?}` and was stored as invalid_observation, so event 0 is deliberately not cited. A revision of the same probe with JSON-safe rendering was therefore submitted for execution, verified locally (both lines now parse; the test still passes); once its receipt is in, the citation can move from explained to holds.\"}"}}
